<ContentPage title="The Vision" subtitle="What we're building — and why it matters.">
  <p>
    BARK — the Bolivian Ark — is a semi-autonomous community in the Yungas region of Bolivia, designed to
    thrive in uncertain times while contributing to the local and global economy. It's not a survivalist compound.
    It's a living laboratory for a better way of life.
  </p>

  <h2>A Community, Not a Compound</h2>
  <p>
    We envision a place where technology serves humanity — not the other way around. Where open-source principles
    govern not just software, but community decisions. Where local knowledge meets global innovation.
  </p>
  <p>
    BARK integrates five pillars into a coherent whole: a <strong>techno park</strong> for research and making,
    a <strong>startup incubator</strong> to grow ventures, a <strong>trading house</strong> for economic self-sufficiency,
    a <strong>technoshamanic retreat center</strong> for healing and culture, and <strong>semi-autonomous infrastructure</strong>
    for energy, water, food, and connectivity.
  </p>

  <h2>Open Source, Open Community</h2>
  <p>
    Everything we build — from solar power systems to governance frameworks — is documented and shared openly.
    This is a project for humanity, not a private estate. Our architectural plans, technical specs, agricultural
    experiments, and governance models are all open for anyone to study, critique, and replicate.
  </p>

  <h2>Integrated, Not Isolated</h2>
  <p>
    BARK doesn't exist apart from the Coroico community — it exists within it. Local employment, cultural exchange,
    shared infrastructure, and economic integration are core principles. We bring resources and technology;
    the local community brings knowledge, culture, and roots. Together, we build something neither could alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Prepared for Anything</h2>
  <p>
    From mild economic turbulence to deeper systemic disruptions, BARK is designed to sustain a good quality of life
    across scenarios. Not through hoarding — through capability. Energy independence, food production, local manufacturing,
    and a community with the knowledge to maintain and adapt its own systems.
  </p>

  <div class="cta-box">
    <p class="font-display font-semibold text-bark-800 mb-2">Want to help build this?</p>
    <p>BARK is seeking co-creators — builders, investors, thinkers, and doers. <a href="/en/participate">See how to get involved</a>.</p>
  </div>
</ContentPage>
